Indeed, inventory management is important for scrawny creatures like
the wizard. But a careful player should be able to balance her
inventory so that she'll have all of the items she needs to reach a
certain point [at your stage, the Quest or the Castle] without pushing
the limit of inventory storage or weight. Review what items you're
carrying.

*Are you keeping 0-charge or less important wands, like wands of
striking and magic missile?
*Have you engrave-tested your wands? Any wand that doesn't yield a
message isn't worth its weight in your main inventory.
*How many potions are you bringing around? Potions that are immediately
useful should be quaffed immediately, like gain level and gain ability.
Potions that waiting to be diluted should wait patiently in your stash.
*Potions of foo-healing in your main inventory should be limited to one
or two. The rest should be kept at your stash, or quaffed for the max
hitpoints bonus.
*Are you lugging around food rations? If you're not doing a vegan or
vegetarian conduct, you should have more than enough food to eat, even
with a dragon.
*Do you have spellbooks and weapons in your main inventory? A wizard
doesn't need more than 5-10 daggers. Any non-crucial spellbooks should
be left behind; highly useful ones like identify and charm monster
should be kept in a stash.
*What sort of armor are you wearing? Drop the shield, the helmet, and
the crystal plate mail.

Before reaching the Castle, I rarely have inventory problems, or a bag
of holding. A lot of the stuff you think you need, you'll find out that
you really don't. Keep the basics, and if you get in trouble, retreat
and restock.



